#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "963f66a5-f742-5b3b-8ae1-f0dbf268bff0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded Between a Curve and the Y-Axis by First Rewriting the Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a6007b2-1915-5162-b380-0c13e4581369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337310} {:answer/id #uuid "db68eb30-cb54-5370-99c0-6b8de6320155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337311} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2a01ef-7dea-584e-a566-a6af37e1fbf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337312} {:answer/id #uuid "0c4163d9-ef06-55ee-9099-c216494d24ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337313} {:answer/id #uuid "28fe86e6-f058-5fb2-9698-01fc50401b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c4163d9-ef06-55ee-9099-c216494d24ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337313} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ac910ea-ce2f-5316-b11e-358af9b1341b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2c8f25f-d60e-58d8-adfc-09591d2ab6e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-10032" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059137/assets/9e19c1cd8bca76f1eec39acfa1b09c0f08b67faea5ddce8325e093923d21d394.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x+1},$ the $y$-axis and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{x+1} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =x+1 \\\\ x & ={y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}-y{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-0)-(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is $|-\\frac{3}{4}|=\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "963f66a5-f742-5b3b-8ae1-f0dbf268bff0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded Between a Curve and the Y-Axis by First Rewriting the Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8500aa29-4719-5c4c-a3ca-f35c6ede931f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8500aa29-4719-5c4c-a3ca-f35c6ede931f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337317} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb364830-507a-582d-82ce-38cf323a5600" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c61a2e55-9279-560a-a21e-c4a0618f9e54" :question/math-academy-id "q-346783" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059137/assets/816b36f644f3e41d8f48bed2dd49e150d751cb13e082b6a1b9b4ad16973d3d57.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ between the curve $y=3\\sqrt{\\frac{x+3}{3}}$, the $y$-axis and the lines $y=1$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{\\frac{x+3}{3}} \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{x+3}{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{x+3}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} & =x+3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-3)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-3{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,|}_{1}^{2}-3\\cdot y\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{3})-3(2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}-3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So, the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-\\frac{20}{9}|=\\frac{20}{9}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384ce5b6-40da-59a0-bc87-a1d726328cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Finite Area Bounded Between a Curve and the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a23a8f4-deb3-5a57-95f0-17ad16d1eac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337320} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed6ddb7-d571-5dd3-83ae-8a4ab24d7a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337321} {:answer/id #uuid "392b5bf6-ac20-51cc-a18b-aa3d9ed320c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337322} {:answer/id #uuid "2b29bad7-a690-5677-a992-4dce2b94dc81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337323} {:answer/id #uuid "63b6008d-355a-5d97-bf38-fe33df8a1f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "392b5bf6-ac20-51cc-a18b-aa3d9ed320c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337322} :answer-field/id #uuid "92143d1e-75a1-5e86-90e1-1ec3bf84ab21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94aef3ce-716b-5bd0-a343-fed185ba9a2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-78888" :question/problem "Find the area of the closed region between the curve $x=16{y}^{4}-1$ and the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{y}^{4}-1 & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{4} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{4} & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059137/assets/c1e092dd6302bb5948c6452e43b093fc3a531132580c1bb1e42fbe846152f63c.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=16{y}^{4}-1$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ to $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}(16{y}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(16{y}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =32\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}|}_{0}^{1/2}-2\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}(\\frac{1}{32}-0)-2(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $|-\\frac{4}{5}|=\\frac{4}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384ce5b6-40da-59a0-bc87-a1d726328cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Finite Area Bounded Between a Curve and the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b87da0b6-309e-5f28-b180-65f154632e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337327} {:answer/id #uuid "72ddbc94-0650-5233-8dec-b9fffd42d3be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337328} {:answer/id #uuid "c35d5824-5a15-5158-ab70-a59a2f17ded4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337329} {:answer/id #uuid "3ff44da7-7f0b-5e31-adc9-2482cc0ee02a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337330} {:answer/id #uuid "719eb23c-0837-594a-94ff-067e1395eefe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "719eb23c-0837-594a-94ff-067e1395eefe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337331} :answer-field/id #uuid "9415b612-7882-5f74-b7c4-26c49630918d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "395038c5-fc04-5903-a4d5-851b4cfb7b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-78891" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059137/assets/a45999ec28d309b3be249304e9b5175f66f2b862cd7f18f2c51b3182333e9a08.png)\n\nFind the area of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =0 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that we want to find the area between the curve $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "963f66a5-f742-5b3b-8ae1-f0dbf268bff0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded Between a Curve and the Y-Axis by First Rewriting the Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9fe06f2-03ed-5a80-a771-512cbecc711a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337334} {:answer/id #uuid "a3943668-28c0-51e5-90aa-e780d1a35f9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337335} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb50ace-e763-5639-ae0d-60fa29ee35ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337336} {:answer/id #uuid "20f43e69-03aa-5490-9222-b9cfc44eb21e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337337} {:answer/id #uuid "144dc1d9-5b0d-592d-b9f0-97c86170563e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20f43e69-03aa-5490-9222-b9cfc44eb21e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337337} :answer-field/id #uuid "e763c6a3-91a7-525d-947f-20dd723a946e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50dcb5d3-19b7-51a7-b9f1-2c4840ac90d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-78897" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059137/assets/cc0f19d50ccd27918d0ac005dec9a8cd27a4f104726ded0d07c71b571ad0522f.png)\n\nFind the area between the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-x}$, the $y$-axis and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{4-x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4-x \\\\ x & =4-{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(1-0)-(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
